2026-09-21 13:03:17 [DEBUG] llm.engine: ARGS: ('\n        You are a workflow assistant. Extract parameters for the intent "select_idea_by_reference".\n\n        User message:\n        select this: 20-601 cameron\n\n        Parameter schema for this intent:\n        - "reference": type=string, required=True\n\n        Extraction instructions for this intent:\n        - The "reference" parameter must always be set to the idea\'s "id" field.\n- Never return the idea name or description.\n- Use the relevant context block (which lists ideas with id, name, and description)\n  to determine which idea the user is referring to.\n- If the user refers to an idea by name, description, or position (e.g., "the immigration case",\n  "idea 3", "the fed reserve governor case"), resolve it to the correct idea and return its id.\n\n\n\nRelevant context:\nideas:\n  - id: 25-197, name: T. M. v. Univ. of MD Medical Sys. Corp., description: The Supreme Court is being asked to clarify the scope of a legal doctrine known as Rooker-Feldman, which limits the jurisdiction of lower courts to challenge final decisions from state high courts.\n  - id: 20-1573, name: Viking River Cruises, Inc. v. Moriana, description: The Supreme Court is deciding whether a state law claim can be forced into arbitration, as agreed upon by the parties involved, or if it is exempt from this agreement due to its nature.\n  - id: 24-320, name: Soto v. United States, description: The Supreme Court is deciding whether the Combat-Related Special Compensation statute grants the secretary concerned authority to settle claims, making the Barring Act inapplicable. The dispute centers on whether the statute authorizes a specific government official to determine claim validity and amount due, which would exempt it from the Barring Act.\n  - id: 20-601, name: Cameron v. EMW Women’s Surgical Center, description: The case concerns the Attorney General\'s ability to intervene in a lawsuit on behalf of Kentucky, particularly after another state official had stopped defending House Bill 454. The issue revolves around the Attorney General\'s capacity to participate in the case and whether they are jurisdictionally barred from doing so. The dispute centers on the Commonwealth\'s sovereign interests in enforcing and defending its law.\n  - id: 22-1025, name: Gonzalez v. Trevino, description: The case of Gonzalez v. Trevino, docket number 22-1025, concerns the interpretation of a Supreme Court ruling known as Nieves, which protected on-the-street law enforcement officers from lawsuits. The issue is whether this protection extends to government officials who punish their critics, and if it limits the types of evidence that can be used in such lawsuits.\n  - id: 21-1454, name: OH Adjutant Gen.\'s Dept. v. FLRA, description: The case is about whether the Federal Labor Relations Authority has the power to issue orders to state guards and adjutants general, who employ technicians, or if they are exempt from the Authority\'s jurisdiction under a federal law known as the Reform Act.\n  - id: 21-1052, name: U.S., ex rel. Polansky v. Executive Health, description: The government\'s authority to dismiss a False Claims Act case after declining to proceed is at issue in this Supreme Court case, with the government arguing it has such authority and the relator disagreeing.\n  - id: 23-1067, name: Oklahoma v. EPA, description: The case Oklahoma v. EPA concerns the jurisdiction of federal courts to review Environmental Protection Agency actions on state implementation plans, specifically whether a single national action can be considered when multiple states\' plans are disapproved together.\n  - id: 18-776, name: Guerrero-Lasprilla v. Barr, description: The case concerns the scope of judicial review over questions of law decided by the Board of Immigration Appeals, specifically whether courts can review whether the government used the proper legal standard in making decisions.\n  - id: 19-177, name: Agency for Int’l Development v. Alliance for Open Society Int’l, Inc., description: The case is about whether the US government can require foreign organizations receiving AIDS relief funding to have a policy opposing prostitution and sex trafficking, despite the Supreme Court previously ruling that this condition cannot be applied to domestic organizations.\n  - id: 19-547, name: United States Fish and Wildlife Serv. v. Sierra Club, Inc., description: The Supreme Court is considering whether the government must disclose internal documents related to a decision made in 2014 about the impact of a proposed action on endangered species. The documents were written before the final decision was made and may not have been adopted by the decision-makers.\n  - id: 23-1226, name: McLaughlin Chiropractic Assoc. v. McKesson Corp., description: The case concerns the interpretation of a federal law, the Hobbs Act, which governs direct review of agency orders in courts of appeals. Petitioners argue that the Act does not require district courts to treat agency orders as binding precedent, while respondents urge a broader interpretation that would bind courts to agency interpretations of federal statutes.\n  - id: 19-7, name: Seila Law LLC v. Consumer Financial Protection Bureau, description: The case is about a dispute over the government\'s ability to enforce a Civil Investigative Demand against Seila Law LLC, with the company arguing that it is not moot and has standing to appeal.\n  - id: 23-21, name: Harrow v. Dept. of Defense, description: The Supreme Court is deciding whether a specific filing deadline in the Harrow v. Dept. of Defense case is a procedural rule or a requirement for the court\'s jurisdiction, with implications for how appeals are handled.\n  - id: 21-86, name: Axon Enterprise, Inc. v. FTC, description: The Supreme Court is considering whether Congress has the authority to allow district courts to hear constitutional claims related to agency officials\' removal and access to the courts, despite a separate grant of jurisdiction to appeals courts for unrelated orders.\n\nFollow these rules:\n- Extract parameters strictly according to the schema.\n- Apply the extraction instructions above.\n- Use context only when the user refers indirectly (e.g., "it", "that one").\n- If a parameter cannot be extracted, set it to null.\n- Return ONLY valid JSON. No commentary.\n\nReturn JSON in this format:\n{\n    "parameters": {\n        <param_name>: <value or null>\n    },\n    "reasoning": "<brief explanation>"\n}\n',)
