2026-09-21 13:09:42 [DEBUG] llm.engine: ARGS: ('\n        You are a workflow assistant. Extract parameters for the intent "select_idea_by_reference".\n\n        User message:\n        select this: Free Speech Coalition v. Paxton\n\n        Parameter schema for this intent:\n        - "reference": type=string, required=True\n\n        Extraction instructions for this intent:\n        - The "reference" parameter must always be set to the idea\'s "id" field.\n- Never return the idea name or description.\n- Use the relevant context block (which lists ideas with id, name, and description)\n  to determine which idea the user is referring to.\n- If the user refers to an idea by name, description, or position (e.g., "the immigration case",\n  "idea 3", "the fed reserve governor case"), resolve it to the correct idea and return its id.\n\n\n\nRelevant context:\nideas:\n  - id: 21-1333, name: Gonzalez v. Google LLC, description: The case concerns the interpretation of a federal law that shields internet companies from liability for content created by others, with petitioners arguing the court should adopt a broad rule that certain recommendations are inherently protected.\n  - id: 22-555, name: NetChoice, LLC v. Paxton, description: The case involves a challenge to a Texas law that regulates social media platforms, with the plaintiffs arguing it is facially unconstitutional due to content-based discrimination and interference with their First Amendment rights.\n  - id: 20-1029, name: Austin v. Reagan National Advertising, description: This case, Austin v. Reagan National Advertising, involves a dispute over the interpretation of content-based regulation under the First Amendment and whether certain sign codes are constitutional.\n  - id: 19-631, name: Barr v. American Assn. of Political Consultants, Inc., description: The case concerns a federal law restricting automated calls to cell phones, with the petitioners arguing that an exception for government-debt calls undermines the law\'s consumer protection function and violates the First Amendment.\n  - id: 21-476, name: 303 Creative LLC v. Elenis, description: The central dispute in this case is whether a public accommodations law that requires businesses to provide equal access to their services violates the First Amendment. The company claims that requiring them to serve gay couples infringes on their expression, but the law actually targets discriminatory sales and its effect on expression is incidental. If granted a pre-enforcement order, the company could turn away all gay couples and potentially empower other businesses to refuse service based on various characteristics.\n  - id: 22-611, name: Lindke v. Freed, description: The case of Lindke v. Freed concerns whether a public official\'s use of a private social media account to communicate with constituents is subject to constitutional scrutiny, particularly when the official blocks users from that channel.\n  - id: 24-656, name: TikTok, Inc. v. Garland, Att\'y Gen., description: The case concerns a federal law that would force the popular social media platform to shut down in nine days, with one of its main arguments being that it restricts the company\'s free speech.\n  - id: 23-411, name: Murthy, Surgeon Gen. v. Missouri, description: The case concerns the government\'s ability to communicate with and about social media platforms, with petitioners arguing that a lower court\'s injunction restricting government speech was based on legal errors and should be reversed.\n  - id: 23-1122, name: Free Speech Coalition v. Paxton, description: The Supreme Court is considering a challenge to a Texas law that restricts access to sexually themed websites, arguing that it unfairly burdens constitutionally protected speech and could set a precedent for future regulations that imperil free speech online.\n  - id: 20-255, name: Mahanoy Area School Dist. v. B. L., description: The case centers on whether the landmark Supreme Court decision Tinker applies to speech that occurs off school grounds, particularly on social media, and whether schools can restrict such speech without infringing on students\' First Amendment rights.\n  - id: 22-324, name: O’Connor-Ratcliff v. Garnier, description: The case O\'Connor-Ratcliff v. Garnier concerns whether public officials retain their First Amendment rights when acting in a personal capacity, specifically when blocking users from their private social media pages.\n  - id: 20-804, name: Houston Community College Sys. v. Wilson, description: The Supreme Court is considering whether a member of an elected body can sue for retaliation after being censured by their peers, with the issue centered on the First Amendment\'s protection of free speech versus the right to criticize and respond to a member\'s actions.\n  - id: 21-1496, name: Twitter, Inc. v. Taamneh, description: Twitter, Inc. is suing over a law that allows U.S. citizens injured by terrorism to sue companies that knowingly helped terrorists, but the company claims it did not knowingly aid ISIS and only enforced policies against terrorist activity.\n  - id: 19-251, name: Americans for Prosperity Foundation v. Bonta, description: The case is about whether California\'s requirement that charities disclose their top donors nationwide to the state Attorney General is constitutional, as it allegedly chills free speech and is not narrowly tailored for law enforcement purposes.\n  - id: 22-277, name: Moody v. NetChoice, LLC, description: The case, Moody v. NetChoice, LLC, concerns whether internet platforms have a First Amendment right to censor user speech and deplatform users when their policies contradict their own representations.\n\nFollow these rules:\n- Extract parameters strictly according to the schema.\n- Apply the extraction instructions above.\n- Use context only when the user refers indirectly (e.g., "it", "that one").\n- If a parameter cannot be extracted, set it to null.\n- Return ONLY valid JSON. No commentary.\n\nReturn JSON in this format:\n{\n    "parameters": {\n        <param_name>: <value or null>\n    },\n    "reasoning": "<brief explanation>"\n}\n',)
